# THE DEFAULT, ADDED THE FIFTH TIME THIS BUG WAS FOUND.
#
# Everything below this line is the record of the same failure happening four
# times: a golden file, a generated contract, the shell scripts, the parsed
# markdown. Each was fixed by pinning one more pattern, and each fix worked --
# for the pattern it named. The fifth was .github/workflows/sonar.yml, read by
# internal/testenv/sonar_gate_test.go with strings.Index(wf, "\n  sonar:\n").
# Checked out with CRLF that anchor matches nothing, so the guard reported the
# `sonar` job "has been renamed" -- about a file nobody had touched, on
# windows-latest and nowhere else.
#
# Pinning a sixth pattern would be the same fix a fifth time, and it relies on
# the next person writing the next guard remembering that this class of bug
# exists. It does not survive that. So the default is inverted: no tracked text
# file is checked out with CRLF, and a new file type inherits the fix instead of
# rediscovering it.
#
# `text=auto` still leaves genuinely binary files alone -- git detects those and
# does not touch their bytes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one deliberate exception. These run under Windows PowerShell, which is the
# single consumer in this repository that has a real reason to want CRLF, and
# nothing parses them byte for byte.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What follows is now redundant with the default above. It is kept because it
# is the evidence for it: five incidents is why the default exists, and a
# future reader deciding whether the blanket rule is worth its breadth should
# be able to read what it replaced.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows checks text files out with CRLF by default. That is harmless
# for anything this repository compiles or parses, and NOT harmless for a file
# compared byte for byte.
#
# internal/engine/testdata/selector_golden.txt is exactly that: 3200 rows
# freezing every reachable answer of chooseSource, asserted against the running
# code as whole bytes. Checked out with CRLF it fails on windows-latest and
# nowhere else, reporting "3200 of 3200 rows changed" -- every row differing
# only by an invisible \r. Worse, the diff it prints to justify that claim
# arrives BLANK, because the carriage return sends the cursor back over the line
# just written. The failure therefore accuses the selector of a total rewrite
# and shows nothing to support it, which is the most expensive shape a CI
# failure can take.
#
# Pinned by directory rather than by name so the next golden file inherits the
# fix instead of rediscovering the bug.
**/testdata/** text eol=lf

# ui/src/lib/contract/ is the same problem one directory over, and it found
# this file the hard way: the schedule-action contract is generated by a Go
# test and compared byte for byte against the committed copy, so a CRLF
# checkout failed on windows-latest and nowhere else -- printing a "got" and a
# "want" that were, to the eye, character for character identical. A diff that
# looks like it proves the test wrong is worse than no diff.
#
# By directory again, for the reason given above: the next contract file
# inherits the fix rather than rediscovering it. These are also read by vitest
# on the UI side, which does not care either way.
ui/src/lib/contract/** text eol=lf

# The acceptance suites and their shared libraries run under bash on Linux and
# macOS runners. A CRLF checkout makes the shebang line end in \r, and the
# kernel then looks for an interpreter literally named "bash\r" -- reported as
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory", which reads as a missing shell
# rather than a line-ending problem. Nothing runs these on Windows today; this
# is here so that stays true by construction rather than by luck.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The third time. docs/PLATFORMS.md, docs/API.md and docs/SITE-DEPLOY.md are
# all PARSED BY GO TESTS -- the capability table, the endpoint list and the
# deploy steps are each compared against the running code -- which makes the
# claim at the top of this file ("harmless for anything this repository
# compiles or parses") no longer true of markdown.
#
# What it cost: the header pattern in platforms_doc_drift_test.go was anchored
# on ` *$`, which does not match a line ending in \r, so it found no header at
# all and reported that the capability table "has moved or been renamed". On
# windows-latest and nowhere else, about an intact document, five minutes after
# the push. A failure that accuses the wrong file is worse than a slow one.
#
# By pattern rather than by directory this time: the next drift test may read a
# README, and the point of pinning is that it inherits the fix. Nothing renders
# markdown differently for a \r, so there is no cost on the other side.
*.md text eol=lf
